By and large, scarcity is not a good thing. I can argue convincingly
that farming encourages scarcity, because it requires designers to
make important items that can be generated randomly *more* scarce than
they would be without that random means of generation.

Scarcity, when it leads to wandering around killing time looking for
that one last item, is *no fun*. I had one game of Angband where I had
killed every single unique in the dungeon except for Morgoth, but
after a few attempts to put him away, I concluded there was no way I
could finish without one last speed item. They are incredibly rare in
the game, and I believe I burned up one or two million turns (100,000
turns in Angband is *nothing*) over a period of days, looking mostly
for Cubragol before I finally, and incredibly unsatisfyingly, just
gave up. I won a couple of others, but the fact that I wasn't able to
win this game for no reason other than some whim of the RNG really
stuck in my craw.

So, on the one hand, I oppose farming when it leads to the situation
above, where a significant source of important items is random
generation, and because of the possibilities of farming/polypiling,
designers make the decision to make those items incredibly rare in
order to increase the "difficulty" of the game - but tedium is a very
shallow difficulty.

On the other hand, I endorse farming, polypiling, and their spiritual
kin when they are a means of obtaining important items that aren't of
unique significance, or of doing mundane things like increasing HP,
SP, $$, and XP when those aren't really the linchpin of the game.
Also, I endorse them because they have some *fun* value. Turning a
pile of useless crap into different potentially less useless crap is
just plain fun.

Basically, I believe that farming in NetHack is more or less as it
should be. There are items in the game that are of unique
significance, but I am not sure there should be more, or that the game
should be completely unforgiving of failed attempts to acquire such
new items. Of *course* you should be able to lose the game late during
play, but the kinds of failure you should experience then should be of
an entirely different nature than those earlier in the game. They
should be "oops, I *knew* I shouldn't have done that" and not "oh, no,
I forgot to do something 8 hours ago, I've been screwed this whole
time" or "what, did I just hit the wrong key?" Bad long term strategy,
with very few exceptions, should be fixable, should you abruptly
become a brilliant strategist, because that gives you the opportunity
to *enjoy* getting out of a jam, telling a good story, and so on.
Being short an item late in the game and not being able to work around
it, that is no fun, particularly if it has been a long and for the
most part correctly and carefully played game.

This has wandered. What I am hoping to get across is that the issues
associated with farming and other "generate lots of random objects in
hoping of getting some good ones" strategies are subtle and sometimes
counterintuitive. In another community of mine, we call this the "XY
problem." The "XY problem" is someone asking how to do X because he
actually wants to do Y, but X is the wrong way to do it. I strongly
believe that what most people view as solution X, "stop pudding
farming," is really a misguided attempt at solving a different problem
Y. Unfortunately, problem Y isn't agreed upon or perhaps even known,
and whatever the solution to it is, it isn't X.



